    "input": "{\"current_utc\": \"2026-09-24T17:21:55.374262+00:00\", \"assigned_topic\": {\"study_topic_id\": \"study-4d6dd331abd2f9ce3137\", \"subject_id\": \"subject-d59c6ae175416df6dd68\", \"topic\": \"Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz\", \"primary_category\": \"Social Science and Philosophy\", \"description\": \"German polymath and rationalist philosopher known for the doctrines of monads and pre-established harmony, the claim that this is the best of all possible worlds, and the independent development of calculus.\", \"subcategory_ids\": [\"social-002\", \"social-007\"], \"level_occurrence_counts\": {\"high-school\": 2}, \"role_occurrence_counts\": {\"answer\": 2}, \"source_question_count\": 2, \"aliases\": [\"Gottfried Leibniz\", \"Leibniz\", \"Gottfried Wilhelm von Leibniz\", \"Gottfried Wilhelm Leibnitz\"], \"topic_type\": \"person\", \"source_ids\": [\"qb-f2fddb73041830b7dc9e5de6\", \"qb-9ba48bb11c8c13c1ac8b226a\"], \"source_questions\": [{\"custom_id\": \"qb-f2fddb73041830b7dc9e5de6\", \"level\": \"high-school\", \"question_type\": \"tossups\", \"source\": \"2022 SHOW-ME\", \"round\": \"3\", \"number\": \"16\", \"parts\": [{\"label\": \"tossup\", \"question\": \"This philosopher imagined walking inside a thinking machine as if it were a mill to argue that physical mechanisms cannot explain human consciousness. This non-Democritus thinker proposed shapeless units of reality which each have different qualities, with the soul consisting of a dominant one of those units. In his Théodicée, this philosopher attempted to solve the problem of evil by stating that we live in the (*) “best of all possible worlds.” This author of Monadology devised the modern notation for mathematical derivatives. For 10 points, name this German polymath who invented calculus independently of Isaac Newton.\", \"answer\": \"ANSWER: Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz [LYBE-nits]\"}]}, {\"custom_id\": \"qb-9ba48bb11c8c13c1ac8b226a\", \"level\": \"high-school\", \"question_type\": \"bonuses\", \"source\": \"2022 IQBT Regular Season Set 1\", \"round\": \"8\", \"number\": \"18\", \"parts\": [{\"label\": \"L\", \"question\": \"This German philosopher's Theodicee (thee-oh-deet-SEE) proposes that we live in the “best of all  possible worlds.” For 10 points each:\", \"answer\": \"\"}, {\"label\": \"A\", \"question\": \"Name this philosopher who is satirized in Candide with the character Pangloss.\", \"answer\": \"Gottfried Wilhelm von Leibniz [or Gottfried Wilhelm Leibnitz]\"}, {\"label\": \"B\", \"question\": \"Leibniz theorized the existence of these “windowless” nonspatial particles in a treatise titled for  their “-ology.”\", \"answer\": \"monads [or Monadology]\"}, {\"label\": \"C\", \"question\": \"The Monadology's doctrine of “pre-established harmony” resolves a problem arising from the idea  that mind and body are separate substances, or this kind of “dualism.” This adjective, derived from the  name of a French polymath, also describes the x-y coordinate system.\", \"answer\": \"Cartesian\"}]}]}}",
